# T-001 — stack research recommendation

Status: complete. Date: 2026-10-17. Owner: Codex Director `director-test`.

**GO for A; conditional GO for B as disposable capability spikes. Neither is approved as the final stack. Start both with the mandatory precision-touchpad probe; stop a stack early if it needs a substantial input/editor rewrite.** A has the clearer editable inline-control extension seam and lower local setup friction. B has a concrete Windows Direct Manipulation pixel-input path, but its public end phase is not established as finger lift. No performance winner can be selected from documentation.

Evidence: [Microsoft options](../../docs/research/first-party.md), [third-party/editor/carousel assessment](../../docs/research/third-party.md). Both contain dated primary-source links and explicit unknowns.

## Decisions

| Candidate | Recommendation | Reason / first gate |
|---|---|---|
| A: Avalonia + AvaloniaEdit | GO, input gate first | Documented materials/tray; editor source demonstrates inline controls and styling infrastructure. Windows PTP wheel events lack a proven release lifecycle; prove native lifecycle integration before expanding the spike. |
| B: GPUI + GPUI Kit | Conditional GO, input and editor gates first | Maintained toolkit with editor features and a Windows pixel-pan implementation. Verify the exact pinned source, contact-to-inertia transition, wrapped clickable checkbox extension and native tray/hotkey integration. |
| WinUI/WPF custom editor | NO-GO for another general editor spike | Stock rich-text controls do not remove the Markdown source/widget/lexer synchronization work. WinUI's materials and InteractionTracker remain useful targeted native components if the carousel requires them. |
| WinUIEdit | NO-GO as a preferred replacement | Third-party Scintilla wrapper; explicit readiness warning, prerelease package, no demonstrated advantage for Markdown widgets. |
| C / another stack | Keep C as backup; no additional spike recommended | This review found no established option that closes both editor and exact Windows PTP release requirements more convincingly. This is a scoped finding, not an exhaustive ecosystem survey. |

These are research recommendations; the manager still owns the stack decision. The user's prior custom-editor cost concern is technically supported by the missing stock capabilities; no elapsed-effort estimate is claimed.

